From 3cc22ea8f0bbc4b1d1561c973c282374dfbe4870 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yun Dou <dixyes@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 15:39:22 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 5/8] arm64: Send a site that keeps trapping to a block

A site that runs an atomic operation in a loop pays an exception every
time, which is a million of them to boot a distribution. A site that has
trapped often enough is therefore sent to a block that makes the
operation without one, and the emulation stays where it is as the way
back for anything the block cannot finish.

The block is a few instructions in a page of the task's own, placed where
the branch from the site reaches it and chosen by the mm rather than
fixed: the mapping is asked for half a window away from the site, since a
page put beside it is one the heap and the loader grow into next. The
block saves the two registers it works in on the stack in one store,
which is the first thing it does so that a fault on it leaves the task
exactly as it was, makes the operation with the exclusive pair, and
branches back to the instruction after the site. The store of that pair
is the releasing one, as the emulation of the same instruction uses, or a
task taking a lock would see the lock taken before what it protects was
written.

Everything a fault inside the block needs is in the block itself, so
there is no state in the kernel to keep: what the instruction was, where
it was, what the frame holds and how long the block is are all written
next to the code. The page is told apart from the mappings around it by
the offset field of the mapping, together with two flags that say nothing
about an anonymous mapping of a task's own and keep it out of the merges
that would take the offset away with them.

A fault taken in a block is the instruction's while the block has not
made the operation yet, and is answered by the emulation of it, with the
registers the frame holds and at the address of the site. The one other
fault that is the instruction's is the branch the block ends with, which
is the instruction after the site and is reported there. The operations
whose result needs a comparison, and the sites whose registers a block
cannot work around, are left to the emulation, which is the way back
either way.

How many traps a site is worth is lse_rewrite, two by default, zero for a
block at the first trap, and below zero for none at all. What was made,
and how often the emulation had to answer for a block, are counted in
<debugfs>/lse_emul/stats. With the blocks in place the demo's LSE atoms
are carried out by 1088 exceptions where they were 32231, and an Arch
boot to a login goes from 359512 to 213096, with 1219 blocks made and 131
of them answered for by the emulation.

